#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079292} :activity/id #uuid "d2b403a1-8031-5a79-8235-5b648d030a5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048338 :topic/id #uuid "96be3b68-ea0a-594d-ac9b-2bd958c09c72" :topic/math-academy-id 669} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079728 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is $6$ and the fifth term is $12.$ Can we use this information to compute the common difference of the sequence?\n\nThe first thing to note is that the sequence is *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/6b/6b82ba58c986aa32635e2ebbd8b1e0fd0affdbc81e35a015960777fa72d5f72b.png)\n\nBecause $a_4 = 6$ and $a_5 = 12$ are **consecutive terms**, meaning that $a_5$ comes straight after $a_4$ in the sequence, we can easily compute the common difference $d$ by subtracting $a_4$ from $a_5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =12-6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e69a6b27-01d4-5ee2-81c3-d7d8f834b087" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8911 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8a0caf7-c1c8-5350-9d6b-8db3c51bdd00" :step/math-academy-id 148381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078183 :question/id #uuid "cb23119e-7625-5dad-873f-03049677618c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9307a5e-2eea-5cea-b5f3-2ce6df53961e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049579}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519575}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "ca7d2f7b-9202-580e-bad9-b64525e020c2" :step/math-academy-id 148382 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the second and fifth terms only of an arithmetic sequence. For example, suppose that we know that\n\n$$\na_2 = {\\color{red}8}, \\quad a_5 = {\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nThese terms are not consecutive, and so how can we figure out the common difference $d?$\n\nFirst, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/ff/ff83c75935acca7ddd5c421cd81fad85685c68fd782d1d832c068237c751753d.png)\n\nWe see that between ${\\color{red}8}$ and ${\\color{red}23}$, there are a total of ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps. So, to find the value of a single jump, we can take the total difference and divide it by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{{\\color{red}23}\\,-\\,{\\color{red}8}}{\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{15}{3} = 5\n$$\n\nWe can check that our result $d=5$ is correct by using it to figure out the intermediate terms. Indeed, the common difference $d=5$ gets us from $a_2={\\color{red}8}$ to $a_5={\\color{red}23}$ in ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps:\n\n![](images/7a/7aa192675aeb3c7be78617035b74362b38974e8501d95c0692238811ae4769f1.png)\n\nIn general, if we're given two terms $a_n$ and $a_m$ of an arithmetic sequence, then we can calculate the common difference using the following formula:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_n-a_m}{n-m}\n$$\n\nUsing this formula to calculate the common difference between $a_{2}=8$ and $a_{5}=23,$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{2}}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-8}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dab8ebfb-480a-5a36-9a12-169a69cd8316" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8913 :tutorial/title "Non-Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "59dd0405-0ca2-5664-bbd2-78d0a305318e" :step/math-academy-id 148386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078185 :question/id #uuid "a22637f2-14e5-55ff-a41b-1f2a7e0eaa73" :question/math-academy-id "e-14390"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d15f023-4061-51ad-b307-013bd06d3ec1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048938}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557252}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "2f2df9f7-ad2a-5436-aa72-25df975ccb0b" :step/math-academy-id 148387}] :activity/title "Finding the Common Difference of an Arithmetic Sequence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078685}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557388} :activity/id #uuid "0b2ee7db-7c41-5b6b-a695-5915c75ef129" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048338 :topic/id #uuid "96be3b68-ea0a-594d-ac9b-2bd958c09c72" :topic/math-academy-id 669} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495383fd-96af-5901-b036-2fc2d4ed846d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556595} {:answer/id #uuid "533597a1-e3f5-5fa1-94dc-91bba990baca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556620} {:answer/id #uuid "54e4cfc3-636f-5122-be78-c9628acb2eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556626} {:answer/id #uuid "670cec8e-0d34-56b0-87fa-070c4615dfb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556673} {:answer/id #uuid "fa518048-206a-54de-a58b-6bc20ddc9f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556673} :answer-field/id #uuid "887edb48-4438-5fb8-9f68-ad1ecb484ac1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56794a38-11f6-536d-bb01-6b7e50ccfb8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51839" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has ${a}_{9}=9$ and ${a}_{22}=178.$ What is the common difference of the sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{9}=9,\\,{a}_{22}=178.\n$$\n\nTo find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=22$ and $m=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{9}}{22-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{178-9}{22-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{13} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=13.$"} :step/id #uuid "8aad04a0-c6e7-572a-a7a1-bb3e0b3a6360" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87ba976-114f-5bc8-9b3d-f528fb5b2b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077259} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1d7240-8bbe-50cf-8edf-7f135f060a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077260} {:answer/id #uuid "e8706c52-3a97-54e9-a8e5-74d26fbd9743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077261} {:answer/id #uuid "2eab5511-577f-5ea6-b64e-f6320e4ed0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077262} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea95db3-ff2b-5f09-931a-2faa481430f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186077263} :answer-field/id #uuid "373aa7b8-6a29-57bc-bcdc-cc742a408c61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ff9d20b-55c8-53ea-a576-66b9dcd459f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-5270" :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its fourth and fifth terms are $1$ and $7$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *positive*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive, we can find the common difference by subtracting ${a}_{4}$ from ${a}_{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =7-1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "bb173ec4-dd61-50f2-8584-c82411d790aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dafe0d7-7167-5df4-9ba9-18802932947e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556567} {:answer/id #uuid "6116e349-e0a5-58a8-8407-21b7d71d2915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556650} {:answer/id #uuid "657ee12f-f6f4-59d4-bac7-11a544c78598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556667} {:answer/id #uuid "d2564f09-d536-5dd1-b262-ee4b42fae3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556937} {:answer/id #uuid "d312637a-fb32-535d-b6c7-1fb61f66a8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556667} :answer-field/id #uuid "618eab75-64d8-512a-bb20-45001701ac97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "028f9fde-1b16-58aa-96b4-26b7759a29ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-51867" :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if its seventh and twenty-second terms are $126$ and $-9$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\n{a}_{7}=126,\\,{a}_{22}=-9.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $d,$ we apply the formula with $n=22$ and $m=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{n}-{a}_{m}}{n-m} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{7}}{22-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)-126}{22-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{-135}{15} \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-9.$"} :step/id #uuid "dae43b25-3e8a-58c9-81da-f3af859a0e75"}] :activity/title "Finding the Common Difference of an Arithmetic Sequence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556407}]}
